
## Project Overview

  Completed a hands-on cybersecurity lab focused on using security tools and threat intelligence resources to identify malicious activity. The lab provided practical experience capturing and analyzing network traffic, investigating suspicious files within a secure sandbox environment, and validating domains, IP addresses, and URLs using industry-standard threat intelligence platforms. The exercises aligned with CompTIA CySA+ (CS0-003) Exam Objective 1.3: using appropriate tools and techniques to determine malicious activity.

## Lab Environment
    ACIDC01: Windows Server 2022 Domain Controller
    ACIDM01: Windows Server 2022 Domain Member Server
    ACIWIN11: Windows 11 Domain Member Workstation
    ACIALMA: Alma Linux Standalone Workstation
    ACIKALI: Kali Linux Standalone Workstation
## Key Accomplishments
    1. Monitoring Network Activity
    
    Used network analysis tools to capture, filter, and inspect traffic in order to identify potentially malicious communications and understand normal network behavior.
        
        Customized the Wireshark interface by creating additional columns for destination addresses and destination ports
        Modified packet display settings and timestamps to improve analysis efficiency
        Captured live network traffic using Wireshark on an Alma Linux system
        Applied filters to isolate HTTP traffic and specific IP address ranges
        Analyzed packet details including source and destination information, protocols, and TCP communication
        Sorted captured traffic by protocol and source address to identify communication patterns
        
        This exercise demonstrated how packet capture and protocol analysis can help security analysts identify unauthorized communications, suspicious traffic patterns, and indicators of compromise within a network. Wireshark's filtering capabilities make it possible to quickly isolate traffic relevant to an investigation.
    
    2. Capturing Network Traffic Using Tcpdump
    
        Used command-line packet capture tools to monitor network activity and save traffic for later analysis.
        
        Identified available network interfaces using tcpdump
        Generated ICMP traffic by initiating ping requests from a Windows workstation
        Captured ICMP packets using tcpdump and exported the results to a packet capture (.pcap) file
        Opened and analyzed captured packet data in Wireshark
        Captured HTTP traffic targeting TCP port 80
        Practiced starting and stopping packet captures using command-line tools
        
        This exercise reinforced how tcpdump can be used in Linux environments to collect evidence during security investigations, support threat hunting activities, and gather network data when graphical tools are unavailable. Captured traffic can be preserved for forensic analysis and incident response efforts.
    
    3. Using a Sandbox for Analyzing Malicious Files
    
        Investigated suspicious files in an isolated environment to safely observe potentially malicious behavior.
        
        Opened Windows Sandbox on a Windows 11 workstation
        Copied a suspicious file into the sandbox environment
        Executed the file in the isolated sandbox
        Observed the file's behavior and resulting system impact
        Verified that any changes made within the sandbox were discarded after restarting the environment
        
        This exercise demonstrated the importance of sandboxing when analyzing unknown files. By executing suspicious code in an isolated environment, analysts can safely observe behavior without risking damage to production systems or organizational assets.
    
    4. Validating Domain Names and IP Addresses
    
        Used open-source intelligence (OSINT) and threat intelligence resources to investigate network indicators.
        
        WHOIS Investigation
        Used Whois.com to investigate IP address 13.107.42.14
        Resolved the IP address and identified ownership information
        Verified that the address belonged to Microsoft
        Investigated the domain www.acilearning.com and reviewed hosting and registration details
        
        This demonstrated how WHOIS records can help analysts identify domain ownership, hosting providers, and infrastructure details associated with suspicious activity.
        
        AbuseIPDB Investigation
        Used AbuseIPDB to investigate the domain comptia.org
        Reviewed abuse reports and reputation data associated with the resolved IP address
        Examined WHOIS information linked to the resolved address
        Verified whether indicators had been previously reported for malicious activity
        
        This exercise highlighted the value of reputation-based threat intelligence sources for validating whether IP addresses or domains have been associated with abuse or malicious behavior.
        
    5. Investigating Indicators with VirusTotal
    
        Used VirusTotal to determine whether IP addresses and URLs had been flagged by security vendors.
        
        Investigated suspicious IP address 196.242.35.45
        Reviewed detection results and threat intelligence provided by multiple security vendors
        Investigated the URL www.practice-labs.com using VirusTotal
        Examined URL reputation information and detailed analysis reports
        Reviewed indicators reported by participating security engines
        
        This exercise demonstrated how VirusTotal can be used to quickly validate indicators of compromise and determine whether domains, URLs, or IP addresses have been identified as malicious by the security community.

